  gonna let it go and and what's  interesting about this this happens two  three four times I would say in just  about every football game yeah where  it's it's bang bang like that and it's  almost never  ever commented on because the subsequent  play doesn't really mean anything well  on Monday night the subsequent play  actually was a big place so now it  becomes a huge talking point though they  out you know Denver is outraged you know  the players are talking about it but  it's it this has been consistently for  enforced this way since I got in the  league in 98 and and I'm certainly way  before that this is not this is not  anything unusual at all did you hear  from the league office and they said  look guys don't call it unless it's  really you know egregious like is that  is that what the directive was  absolutely it is you want the ball to be  clearly on the ground when the eyes get  to the football from going from the play  clock in fact if you did it any other  way  if the back judge called it to  technically they would get downgraded  even though it was at zero at the  instant that that the ball you know that  then the ball was still on the ground if  they threw the flag too quickly and shut  the play down they would be downgraded  in that situation I don't understand  that like why why have a rule that you  can't enforce  I mean it's usually it's look you're  right it is loosely enforced because  it's not like the play clock I mean it's  not like the shot clock in basketball  it's it's it's a technical violation  just like you know lining up you know  illegal formations and and the defense  has a space mask just barely in the  neutral zone it's it's things that that  that you could call constantly  throughout the game but just just  frustrate fans coaches players officials  that don't need to be called unless it's  clearly obvious that a fouls been  committed but you know staying on that  theme then you can call holding on  everyone right okay you can't 